Kvass is a traditionally fermented beverage commonly produced from rye bread or malted grains. Known for its characteristic sweet-and-sour taste, it has a long cultural history across Russia, Ukraine, Belarus, and other Eastern European markets. Depending on the formulation, kvass is sold as a traditional fermented drink or as a modern soft-drink-style beverage.

The market is evolving as manufacturers combine traditional recipes with contemporary flavors, packaging, and distribution strategies. Producers are working to appeal to younger consumers while maintaining the authenticity valued by established customers. Greater retail visibility and opportunities in specialty food outlets, cafés, and restaurants are also helping introduce kvass to new audiences.

